===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|dated}} To eat luncheon.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1844|author=w:Benjamin Disraeli|title=Coningsby
|passage=In the meantime, while ladies are '''luncheoning''' on chicken pie, or coursing in whirling britskas, performing all the singular ceremonies of a London morning in the heart of the season{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To serve luncheon to.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=H. P. Robinson|title=Digressions. V.—The Convention.|journal=w:[[British Journal of Photography|The British Journal of Photography]]|volume=XLIII|issue=1878|location=London|publisher=Henry Greenwood & Co.,{{nb...|Publishers, 2 York Street, Covent Garden}}|date=1 May 1896|page=277|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/britishjournalof43londuoft/page/277/mode/1up|column=1|passage=Towns welcomed us in our hundreds; M.P.’s '''luncheoned''' us, manufacturers turned out their warehouses, decorated them and feasted us; private families tea’d us on their lawns, but we were so true to our art that we always returned to—“papers!”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Public|year=1909|page=109|passage=This gentleman was presented by Colonel C. E. S. Wood, and was entertained here—wined, dined, tead, breakfasted, coffeed and '''luncheoned'''—and we bought his pictures.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The Stanford Illustrated Review|volume=38|issue=13|year=1936|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=GvrmAAAAMAAJ&q=luncheoned|column=1|passage={{...}} entertained him at the Bohemian Club; Edward D. Kneass, Jr., ’18, extended to him the greeting of the San Francisco Press Club; and Frank J. Taylor, ’18, '''luncheoned''' him at the Honolulu Club.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Detroit|journal=w:[[Boxoffice Pro|Boxoffice]]|volume=34|issue=11|publisher=Associated Publications|date=4 February 1939|page=102|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/boxofficejanmar134unse/page/n593/mode/1up|column=2|passage=A number of friends of Mrs. H. M. Richey, wife of H. M., '''luncheoned''' her on Thursday at the Warden Hotel.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Norman F. Carroll|title=[Class Notes] 09|journal=w:Princeton Alumni Weekly|volume=47|issue=19|location=Princeton, N.J.|publisher=w:Princeton University Press|date=21 February 1947|page=15|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zw9bAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA18-PA15|column=3|passage=To soften him up, a group of his fellows '''luncheoned''' him at the Jonathan Club in Los Angeles.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Broadway|journal=w:[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|volume=173|issue=11|location=New York, N.Y.|date=23 February 1949|page=54|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/variety173-1949-02/page/n237/mode/1up|column=2|passage=Mrs. Harry (Sybil) Brand farewell-'''luncheoned''' her New York friends Monday (21), before taking off for Paris with John Haskell, representing TWA, on month’s vacation, first time over.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:[[Charles H. Baker Jr.|Charles H[enry] Baker, Jr.]]|title=The South American Gentleman’s Companion: Being an Exotic Cookery Book; Or, Up & Down the Andes with Knife, Fork & Spoon|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Crown Publishing Group|Crown Publishers, Inc.]]|year=1951|page=66|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/southamericangen0000char/page/66/mode/1up|passage=We won’t try to lie to you for, bro-''ther!'' until that recent noon when Don Fernando Carlés, Number 1 at National City Bank in Buenos Aires, '''luncheoned''' us at this fantastic Jockey Club—and invitations to this fantastic block-square brownstone Mansion in ''Calle Florída'' are nothing to view lightly—we could take our sting-rays or leave ’em alone.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Paramount World|volume=9|year=1963|page=22|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=RSFf89qy1owC&q=luncheoned|passage=The Legal Department's Richard Colby went off to other pastures, so early this month his associates '''luncheoned''' him at Rosoff's, and in the tradition of the profession, all remarks were off the record.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Geo. H. Carver|title=Sports Slices|journal=w:[[The Intelligencer (Belleville)|The Intelligencer]]|location=Belleville, Ont.|date=7 September 1966|page=13|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/intelligencer-september-1966/page/n88/mode/1up|passage=There must have been a chip of the blarney stone in the Irish stew the pretty stewardess '''luncheoned''' us with, for one quipped.}}

